<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Pichincha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1822">1822</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuadorian-war-of-independence">Ecuadorian War of Independence</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Pichincha was fought on 24 May 1822 on the slopes of the Pichincha volcano above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>. Patriot forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a>, including troops sent by Bolívar and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, defeated the royalist army of Melchor Aymerich. Quito surrendered the next day, ending Spanish rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, which joined Gran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The struggle for the Presidencia de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> began on 9 October 1820, when the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> proclaimed its independence after an almost bloodless revolt. A first Patriot advance into the highlands was beaten at the First Battle of Huachi on 22 November 1820. From February 1821 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>, President of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, sent men and supplies, and in May Antonio José de Sucre arrived to take command. Bolívar wanted every province of the Real Audiencia, Guayaquil included, joined to Colombia. Sucre's second campaign also ended in defeat at Huachi, on 12 September 1821, and an armistice followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In January 1822 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">Sucre</a> took an indirect route through the southern highlands. At Saraguro on 9 February he was joined by about 1,500 men of the Peruvian Division under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-de-santa-cruz">Andrés de Santa Cruz</a>, and on 21 February he retook Cuenca without a shot. After a cavalry clash at Tapi, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riobamba">Riobamba</a>, on 21 April, he flanked the Royalist positions along the slopes of the Cotopaxi volcano, and the Royalists fell back on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>.</p><p>On the night of 23 to 24 May the Patriot army of 2,971 men climbed Pichincha, slowed by rain that turned the trails to mud. Royalist sentries saw them at dawn, and Aymerich sent his 1,894 men up the mountain. On steep, broken ground the fighting went badly for the Patriots until the Albión battalion of British volunteers, arriving with the reserve ammunition, stopped the veteran Aragón battalion as it was about to strike their rear. The Colombians of the Alto Magdalena then broke the Royalist line, and at midday Aymerich ordered the retreat. The fighting had lasted no more than three hours.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">Sucre</a> reported 1,100 prisoners and 14 guns taken, with four hundred Royalist and two hundred Patriot dead. Lieutenant Abdón Calderón, who stayed in the fight after four wounds, became with Sucre a symbol of the battle for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>. Bolívar entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> on 16 June 1822, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> was proclaimed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> on 31 July. In 1830 the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> seceded to form the Republic of Ecuador. A monument and museum, La Cima de la Libertad, now stand on the battlefield, where a parade is held every 24 May.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Pichincha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Antonio José de Sucre’s victory at the <b>Battle of Pichincha</b> in May 1822 brought in Quito, and Bolívar annexed the port of Guayaquil in July 1822, shortly before meeting José de San Martín there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Quito’s creole elite formed one of the earliest Spanish American juntas on 10 August 1809, but royalist forces suppressed it, and independence came only with the campaign of Antonio José de Sucre, whose victory at the <b>Battle of Pichincha</b> on 24 May 1822 freed Quito.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its first campaigns failed, but with troops sent by Bolívar under Antonio José de Sucre, and Peruvian reinforcements, the patriots won at <b>Pichincha</b> in May 1822.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-province-of-guayaquil">Free Province of Guayaquil</a></li><li><blockquote>Sucre's victory at <b>Pichincha</b> in 1822 brought Quito into Gran Colombia.</blockquote><a href="/state/royal-audiencia-of-quito">Royal Audiencia of Quito</a></li></ul></section>
